<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wallace Carothers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wallace Hume Carothers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wallace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hummay">Hume</a> Carothers was an American chemist, born in Burlington, Iowa, in 1896, who led organic chemistry at DuPont and is credited with the invention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">nylon</a>. After a PhD at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> in 1924 and teaching posts that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>, he joined DuPont's new fundamental research programme at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilmington-delaware">Wilmington, Delaware</a>, where he chose polymer research. His group helped lay the groundwork for neoprene, and in 1935 Gerard Berchet, under his direction, produced polyamide 6-6, the substance later known as nylon. Troubled by depression since his youth, he took his own life in 1937, sixteen months before the discovery of nylon was announced.</p></div>
